I now have enough evidence to confirm the finding. Line 299 in `filter_engine.py` is the critical issue:

```python
expression = text("json_exists(meta,'$?(@.{} == \"{}\")')".format(key, value))
```

Both `key` (a JSON field name from user-supplied filter input) and `value` (user-supplied filter value) are interpolated directly into the raw SQL string passed to `text()`, without any parameterization or sanitization. This is the Oracle branch of the JSON column filtering code. The `key` comes from user-provided filter parameters (passed via request parameters in the REST API), and while `key` may sometimes be coerced to a model attribute (for known table columns), in `json_meta.py` with `strict_coerce=False`, unrecognized keys remain as plain strings (line 90-92 of filter_engine.py shows that when `strict=False`, the raw word is returned unchanged). This means an attacker-controlled key or value reaching the Oracle JSON path gets injected directly into the SQL text.

